INVALID — do not use these numbers.

Partial run, aborted 2026-08-03. Every server-shaped cell (quic, udt,
tsunami) from the `metro` scenario onward is void, and the client-shaped
cells (favonius, uftp) are not certifiable either.

Cause: kill_servers ran

    sh -c 'pkill -f favonius-daemon; pkill -x quic-bench; ...'

and the `sh -c` process's own argv contains the literal text
"favonius-daemon", so `pkill -f` matched the shell running it. The shell
died at the first command (exit 143) and none of the remaining pkills ever
ran. Favonius daemons were reaped, nothing else ever was.

By the time this was caught, eight tsunamid processes had been spinning at
99.8% CPU for 69 minutes, saturating the server's 100 Mbit tbf: a 10s
sample showed 126 MB sent and 2.8M packets dropped. quic and udt were
recorded as TIMEOUT in scenario after scenario; the CSV blamed the tools.
A fresh instance runs quic on metro at 11.9 MB/s with no trouble.

Kept only as the evidence trail for that diagnosis.
Fixed in bench_netem_fair_v2.sh: no `-f` matching anywhere, per-process
exec, SIGKILL escalation, and a loud warning for survivors.
